From 0335680a55c597f7cc32b72d4a4860d7ba0b73e5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 14:53:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf(multimodal): prefix-cache fastpath under
 --mm-processor-cache-gb 0

Base: 1107 vLLM patch head 320021e3c4dbb202d911ab70e2cd924c75420488.

Rationale:
When serving multimodal workloads on 128 GB unified-memory Sparks with --mm-processor-cache-gb 0 and --enable-prefix-caching, every follow-up turn (Turn 2+) in a multi-turn chat completion conversation previously re-ran three expensive host stages even when Turn 1 already populated the P1 prefix cache or encoder cache for the same mm_hash:
1. ImageMediaIO.load_bytes() ran full PIL pixel decompression (Image.open + image.load() + RGB mode conversion) on every turn before reaching the processor.
2. BaseMultiModalProcessor._cached_apply_hf_processor() saw self.cache is None and fell back to _apply_hf_processor(), running full CPU image padding, normalization, and patch tensor extraction (~10 MB per image) just to recompute the prompt placeholder token expansion.
3. AsyncLLM serialized the full ~10-41 MB pixel/patch tensors over ZMQ IPC to EngineCore on every turn, only for Scheduler._update_from_kv_cache() and strip_covered_mm_data() to discard those tensors on a prefix cache hit before sending SchedulerOutput to the worker.

Introduce a zero-tensor-footprint multimodal prefix-cache fastpath under --mm-processor-cache-gb 0:
1. Use multithreaded blake3(max_threads=blake3.AUTO) in MultiModalHasher.get_hasher().
2. Add a bounded LRU validated-header cache (_VALIDATED_IMAGE_META_CACHE) and _LazyPILImage in ImageMediaIO.load_bytes() so repeat image bytes skip pixel decompression unless pixel access is actually requested.
3. Add a bounded LRU metadata-only cache (_prefix_metadata_cache, ~2 KB/image) on BaseMultiModalProcessor storing only keep_on_cpu fields and ResolvedPromptUpdate token expansions per mm_hash, returning a lazy MultiModalKwargsItem on repeat turns without running the HF image processor.
4. Track P1-resident mm_hash identifiers in AsyncLLM and strip device tensors before ZMQ IPC serialization on Turn 2+, with automatic self-healing in Scheduler/EngineCore/AsyncLLM (MMCacheMissError -> mm_cache_miss_hashes -> P0 lazy materialization and transparent resubmit) if P1 ever evicts both the KV prefix blocks and encoder cache entry.

Round 1 (candidate-55):
candidate-55 did not boot, and its release tests failed 13 tests that
candidate-54 passed. Three defects, each fixed here:
1. Every API server start failed. _run_output_handler defines the
   cache-miss retry coroutine, whose parameter annotation names Callable.
   Python 3.12 evaluates it each time the handler starts, and async_llm.py
   never imported Callable (not on this patch's 1107 base, not on
   candidate-54; the patch added none), so all three tp4-ring boots ended in
   NameError in AsyncLLM.__init__. Callable is now imported from
   collections.abc; Any was already imported.
2. Text-only requests depended on multimodal state. _add_request read the
   renderer's processor cache and the prefix-caching flag before
   request.mm_features, and abort() popped _pending_mm_retries, for every
   request. The submission-cleanup tests build AsyncLLM with the text
   path's attributes only, so 10 tests of
   tests/v1/engine/test_request_submission_cleanup.py and 3 of
   tests/entrypoints/serve/utils/test_api_cancellation.py failed with
   AttributeError, which also kept the rollback's engine abort from being
   sent. __init__ now decides once whether the fast path is on
   (_mm_prefix_fastpath: a multimodal processor, no processor cache,
   prefix caching; off by default at class level). _add_request checks
   request.mm_features first, and abort() reads the retry map only when
   the fast path is on and the map is not empty. Text-only requests never
   reach the renderer or the fast path.
3. A stripped item would have stopped the engine (no request in the API
   server carries one today; see below).
   _verify_or_resolve_stripped_mm_features unpacked two values from
   KVCacheManager.get_computed_blocks, which returns three (blocks,
   computed tokens, shared-prefix boundary), so the first request with a
   stripped item raised ValueError in the engine's input handling. It now
   takes the computed-token count by index, as the scheduler does.

Rollback and abort of multimodal requests:
- The materialization await stays between local registration and the
  engine send, inside add_request()'s rollback: a cancellation or failure
  while items materialize rolls back the registration and every sampled
  child, and the shielded engine abort is unchanged.
- A request whose engine send does not complete leaves no retry entry and
  unmarks the identifiers it marked as sent to P1
  (_prepare_mm_features_for_prefix_cache now returns them). Marks made by
  a delivered request (an earlier turn, or an earlier sampled child) stay,
  as after any abort: P1 verifies a stripped item and returns a retryable
  miss when it lacks it.
- A cache-miss retry whose request is aborted while its items materialize
  is not resubmitted, so the engine runs no request without a client, and
  its items are not marked again.

Not changed here: BaseMultiModalProcessor reads prefix caching from
ctx.vllm_config, and InputProcessingContext carries only model_config and
the tokenizer. In the API server the metadata cache therefore never
engages, items are never lazy, and nothing is sent stripped: every turn
runs the HF image processor and sends full tensors. Of this patch, only the
parallel hasher and the image header cache run there. The PR's bench builds
its own context with vllm_config.

Round 2 (candidate-55):
On the path the API server takes, round 1's patch never engaged, JPEG
requests were slower, and four risks were open. Each is addressed here.
1. The fast path engages in the API server. InputProcessingContext gains
   mm_prefix_metadata_cache (default False), MultiModalRegistry
   .create_processor takes it, and the renderer sets it for its serving
   processor when there is no processor cache, prefix caching is on, the
   frontend has one engine (DP 1 or an external load balancer) and there is
   no KV or EC transfer (BaseRenderer._uses_mm_prefix_fastpath). The
   processor reads only this flag (prefix_metadata_cache_enabled) instead of
   a vllm_config the server's context never carried, and AsyncLLM's fast
   path is on exactly when its processor's is. Any other processor (a
   processor cache, profiling and dummy inputs, a context without the flag)
   is unchanged.
2. JPEG slowdown: two parts of this patch ran with the fast path off and
   slowed the processor's own work; neither changed a result.
   a. Repeat image bytes loaded as _LazyPILImage, which moved their decode
      into the processor's thread. The HF processor's work then took about
      three times the minor page faults (14,500 against 5,000 for a
      1302x1302 JPEG request) and 9 ms more kernel time, on every request
      after the first, including turn 1 of new images. The validated-header
      cache is now on only with the fast path, where a repeat image is
      never decoded (set_validated_image_meta_cache_enabled, from the
      renderer). Without it, loading is as before.
   b. MultiModalHasher hashed with blake3 on every core. The processor's
      thread waits for the pool and is woken onto whichever core is free,
      often a Cortex-A725, where the HF processor's work that follows runs
      up to 1.7x slower (a 1920x1080 PNG: 24 ms on the X925 cores, 41 ms
      with two thirds of it on A725 cores), and a 0.9 MB JPEG saved less
      than the pool's wake-up cost. MultiModalHasher is back to hashing on
      its own thread, as before this patch; mm hashes never depended on it.
   With the fast path on, a request with new images costs no more than
   before: when no item comes from bytes loaded before, the processor runs
   its usual path (HF processor, then the prompt updates) while one helper
   thread computes the items' hashes, so the hash no longer adds to turn 1,
   and records the items' metadata afterwards. The header cache is keyed
   by SHA-256 (the CPU's hash instructions, about 3x blake3's single-thread
   speed here) instead of blake3; new bytes are looked up by a pre-key (a
   hash of their length and first and last 16 KiB), and their full hash
   runs on a helper thread alongside their decode (64 KiB or more).
3. A stripped item whose coverage is lost before the encoder needs it
   (evicted after the engine accepted it, a preemption, a later prefill
   chunk) is a retryable miss at schedule time. The scheduler no longer
   schedules the encoder without data, which made the worker raise
   "Encoder cache miss" and stop the engine: it borrows another live
   request's data, or else records the miss and leaves the request
   unscheduled, and update_from_output finishes it with FINISHED_ERROR and
   mm_cache_miss_hashes. The frontend restarts a request that has returned
   nothing yet, with all of its items' data; one that already returned
   output (preempted after its first token) ends with the error, since a
   restart would repeat that output.
4. Every sampled child keeps its retry materializers. Children share the
   parent's mm_features list; a stripped item is now replaced in a copy of
   the child's own list (dataclasses.replace), so the next child still sees
   the lazy item and strips it with its own materializer.
5. Marks are bounded: SentMMIdentifiers is an LRU of 4096 identifiers
   (MAX_SENT_MM_IDENTIFIERS); a dropped mark only means the item is sent
   with its data again.
6. A cache-miss retry materializes in its own task (AsyncLLM._mm_retry_tasks,
   cancelled at shutdown), not inside the output handler, so other
   requests' outputs are not held up meanwhile. A retry that fails finishes
   its request with an error.
Also: a streaming-input request always sends its items' data; a request
finished by a stop string drops its retry entry; frontend materialization
runs with the processor's torch thread setting; the scheduler's miss record
defaults to none on a scheduler built without __init__ (as the scheduler
tests build some); the unused CED replay lookup in
_verify_or_resolve_stripped_mm_features is removed (the computed-blocks
count is already capped at the replay boundary).
Round 1's fixes, #972's rollback of every registered child and the
shielded engine abort are unchanged.

Quality:
Bit- and token-identical prompt token expansions, mm_hashes, and KV block hashes. Cold Turn 1 requests execute the exact full processor pipeline; warm Turn 2+ requests that hit in P1 prefix or encoder cache skip redundant host pixel decode, tensor normalization, and multi-megabyte ZMQ tensor transfers while automatically self-healing on P1 cache eviction.
Round 1: no change to what is processed or sent: on the PR bench's workloads and through a real AsyncLLM frontend, prompt token ids and mm hashes equal candidate-54's.
Round 2: through a real AsyncLLM frontend with the recipe's flags, prompt token ids and mm hashes equal candidate-54's in every workload and turn, and from turn 2 a repeated image is sent without its tensors (6-25 KB requests instead of 10-41 MB), with no decode and no HF processing. The engine verifies each such item when it is added and again when the encoder needs it; a request that misses is resent with its data, or ends with an error if it had already returned output.

